<svg class="diagram-svg" viewBox="0 0 740 606" role="img" aria-labelledby="bdl31regressionexample-title bdl31regressionexample-desc" data-pagefind-ignore>
  <title id="bdl31regressionexample-title">Gaussian process and Bayesian neural network priors, prior matching and posteriors on a one-dimensional regression problem</title>
  <desc id="bdl31regressionexample-desc">A grid of panels with inputs from minus 14 to 14 on the horizontal axis and function values from about minus 4 to 4 on the vertical axis. Row 1: four sample functions from the reference GP prior, which wiggle with a moderate length-scale, and from three BNN priors with fixed hyper-parameters (Gaussian, hierarchical and normalizing flow), whose samples are nearly constant or change once. Row 2: samples from the three GP-induced (GPi) BNN priors, which wiggle like the GP samples. Row 3 (illustrative decays, not optimization results): the 1-Wasserstein distance against iteration from 0 to 800 for the three priors; it falls from about 30 to about 5, from about 45 with early spikes to about 12, and from about 20 to about 1. Rows 4 and 5 are illustrative depictions, not computed BNN posteriors; only the GP posterior is computed. Row 4: posteriors given 64 noisy data points, four near x = minus 9 and the rest between minus 1.4 and 10 tracing an oscillating curve: the GP posterior has wide credible bands in the gap and outside the data, while the posteriors under the fixed priors have narrow bands there. Row 5: the posteriors under the GPi priors have wide bands in the gap and outside the data, like the GP posterior. Dashed lines are means and shaded areas are 95 percent credible intervals.</desc>
